{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## MEASURED: the failure is PERSISTENT but CONDITIONAL — and correlates with finding count, not ticket identity\n\nSixteen plan-review runs from 2026-08-05 were scanned for `Grammar compilation timed out` and\ncorrelated with their verdict payloads. Result:\n\n| runs | grammar timeouts | verify_failed |\n|---|---|---|\n| **14** runs — e369, 941b, 8390, 2c93, 259b, 465d, 18a4 (x2), 4b9e, 9f26, and others | **0** | none |\n| aec1 round 3 | **2** | **True** |\n| aec1 round 4 (in flight) | **1 so far** | pending |\n\n**Not transient:** 4 known occurrences across 2 tickets (this ticket's own runs on\n[rebar:c827-2a23-cd00-473e] x2, reported secondhand, plus aec1 x2 measured here), and round 4 is\nhitting it again after a FULL plan rewrite. **Not universal:** 14 clean runs the same day, same\nmodel.\n\n### The decisive evidence is a SAME-TICKET comparison\n\n    aec1 review #1  ->  10 findings  ->  0 grammar timeouts  ->  BLOCK (clean verdict)\n    aec1 review #3  ->  15 findings  ->  2 grammar timeouts  ->  INDETERMINATE, verify_failed=True\n\nSame ticket, same model, same day. What changed between them was the PLAN: acceptance criteria went\n6 -> 14 and the document grew. Every clean run measured had **<= 10 findings**; the failing one had\n**15**. Candidate threshold therefore sits between 10 and 15 — testable.\n\nMechanism that fits: 'grammar compilation' is the structured-output schema compiled for constrained\ndecoding, and Pass-2 verify must emit a verdict PER FINDING, so its grammar plausibly scales with\nfinding count.\n\n### The perverse part, offered as a hypothesis rather than a conclusion\n\nThe advisory that drove aec1's rewrite was the P6 'compound AC lines bundle deliverables — split\nthem' finding. Splitting took the plan from 6 criteria to 14, and the finding count from 10 to 15.\n**The gate's own remediation advice is the best candidate for what pushed this review past the\nthreshold that then blocked the ticket.** Following the reviewer's guidance may make the reviewer\nunable to verify you. Consistent with every data point available, but not proven.\n\n### Why this matters beyond one ticket\n\nA gate that fails harder on larger plans penalises exactly the changes that most need review, and it\nfails CLOSED with no partial credit — one provider-side 400 degrades EVERY finding to\n`no-verification`, discarding the ~20 criteria that evaluated fine. In aec1's round 3 that meant\n`blocking: 0` yet unsigned: **nothing was wrong with the plan on the gate's own evidence, and it\nstill could not sign.**\n\n### Caveats, stated so they are not lost\n* The indeterminate count is partly an EFFECT of verify failing, not purely a cause.\n* c827's two failures are taken from that investigation's report, not re-measured here.\n* 16 runs on one day is a small sample; the correlation is the best-supported explanation available,\n  NOT a proven mechanism. Establishing it is this ticket's job.\n* Practical consequence: a 5th retry on the same input should be expected to fail identically.\n  Retrying is not a remedy."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785955519943177001,"uuid":"45f211c0-bc2a-4fb7-b65b-a786b4b16f19"}